 verified. The judge — even the corrupt judge's judge — couldn't lie with the whole town watching.\n\nWhat happened next took four seconds and would be told for forty years. When the dust settled, the corrupt judge's gunmen were down — winged, not killed; Jake Calloway never shot to kill if winging would do — and the corrupt judge stood alone, empty-handed."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Drive', 'text': "the corrupt judge's rustlers hit them at the canyon — the last play of a losing hand. The fight was short and decisive: the town rode together, and together is a word the corrupt judge never understood.\n\nThey reached the railhead with the herds and the deed and their heads high. Red Mesa was solvent. Red Mesa was free. Jake Calloway looked back at the dust of the trail and felt the peculiar ache of a job done.\n\nthe corrupt judge wasn't done — the breed never is. The cattle were driven off in the night, the water rights challenged, the railroad brought in. Red Mesa needed more than a verdict; it needed a future."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'Home Range', 'text': "a strongbox key stayed in the courthouse, under glass, with the iron likeness beside it. Proof. Promise. courage at high noon — carved, at the schoolteacher's insistence, on the frame.\n\nthe corrupt judge was last seen heading east, smaller in the distance. Red Mesa didn't gloat. The frontier doesn't gloat; it just keeps the water clean and the word good.\n\nBut Jake Calloway stayed through the winter, helping the schoolteacher mend fences, teaching the kids to read sign, being — for a while — part of Red Mesa instead of just its defender. It suited them."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Sundown', 'text': "courage at high noon. In Red Mesa, on the trail, at sundown and sunup. The west remembers.\n\nRed Mesa is still there, in the valley, under the big sky. The water runs. The cattle graze. The deed holds. Travelers ask about the iron a strongbox key in the courthouse, and the old-timers tell it true.\n\nSpring came, and Jake Calloway rode out with the thaw, a strongbox key's story told and retold in the saloon. the schoolteacher watched them go from the porch, and didn't wave — waving is for endings, and this wasn't one."}]}

